<title>Widget Repair Work Order</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&family=IBM+Plex+Sans+Condensed:wght@500;600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Serif:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;1,400&display=swap">

<style>
:root{
  --bg:#F4F5F7;
  --surface:#FFFFFF;
  --surface-2:#EDEFF3;
  --ink:#1A1C22;
  --muted:#666B79;
  --rule:#DCE0E7;
  --rule-strong:#C3C9D4;
  --broken:#B06E12;
  --broken-wash:#FBF1DF;
  --working:#14655F;
  --working-wash:#E2F0EE;
  --critical:#9E2A21;
  --critical-wash:#FAE9E7;
  --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(20,24,35,.06),0 8px 24px -16px rgba(20,24,35,.28);
  --sans:"IBM Plex Sans Condensed",ui-sans-serif,"Helvetica Neue",Arial,sans-serif;
  --serif:"IBM Plex Serif",ui-serif,Georgia,"Times New Roman",serif;
  --mono:"IBM Plex Mono",ui-monospace,"SFMono-Regular",Menlo,Consolas,monospace;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){
  :root:not([data-theme="light"]){
    --bg:#15161A; --surface:#1C1E24; --surface-2:#23262E;
    --ink:#E6E8ED; --muted:#989EAC; --rule:#2B2F38; --rule-strong:#3B4049;
    --broken:#E0A94A; --broken-wash:#2A2317;
    --working:#4FB0A8; --working-wash:#152827;
    --critical:#E4695C; --critical-wash:#2B1A18;
    --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.4),0 8px 24px -16px rgba(0,0,0,.7);
  }
}
:root[data-theme="dark"]{
  --bg:#15161A; --surface:#1C1E24; --surface-2:#23262E;
  --ink:#E6E8ED; --muted:#989EAC; --rule:#2B2F38; --rule-strong:#3B4049;
  --broken:#E0A94A; --broken-wash:#2A2317;
  --working:#4FB0A8; --working-wash:#152827;
  --critical:#E4695C; --critical-wash:#2B1A18;
  --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.4),0 8px 24px -16px rgba(0,0,0,.7);
}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--serif);font-size:17px;line-height:1.62;-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}
.wrap{max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 28px 96px}
.col{max-width:70ch}

.mast{padding:60px 0 30px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink)}
.eyebrow{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px 18px;margin-bottom:20px}
h1{font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:clamp(2.3rem,5.5vw,3.5rem);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-.018em;margin:0;text-wrap:balance}
h1 .sub{display:block;color:var(--broken);font-size:.42em;font-weight:600;margin-top:13px}
.standfirst{font-size:1.08rem;color:var(--muted);margin:20px 0 0;max-width:64ch}

h2{font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:700;font-size:1.5rem;letter-spacing:-.012em;margin:58px 0 4px;text-wrap:balance}
h2 .ph{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.6em;font-weight:600;color:var(--broken);margin-right:.7em;letter-spacing:.08em}
h3{font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:600;font-size:1.08rem;margin:30px 0 6px}
p{margin:13px 0}
strong{font-weight:600}
code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em;background:var(--surface-2);padding:.12em .38em;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid var(--rule);white-space:nowrap}
.lede{font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--muted)}

.step{background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:4px;padding:20px 24px;margin:16px 0;box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
.step .hd{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px 14px;align-items:baseline;margin-bottom:2px}
.step .n{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:3px 9px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--surface-2);color:var(--muted);border:1px solid var(--rule-strong)}
.step h3{margin:0;font-size:1.1rem}
.step p{font-size:16px;margin:10px 0 0}
.step ul{margin:10px 0 0;padding-left:20px;font-size:16px}
.step li{margin:6px 0}
.done{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px;margin-top:12px;padding:10px 14px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--working-wash);border-left:3px solid var(--working);color:var(--working)}
.warn{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px;margin-top:12px;padding:10px 14px;border-radius:3px;background:var(--critical-wash);border-left:3px solid var(--critical);color:var(--critical)}

pre{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.7;background:var(--surface-2);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:4px;padding:14px 16px;overflow-x:auto;margin:14px 0}
.scroll{overflow-x:auto;margin:20px 0 6px;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:4px;background:var(--surface)}
table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}
th,td{padding:9px 14px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top}
thead th{font-size:10.5px;letter-spacing:.11em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);font-weight:500;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule-strong);white-space:nowrap}
tbody tr:last-child td{border-bottom:none}
td.ok{color:var(--working);font-weight:600}
td.bad{color:var(--critical);font-weight:600}
.tnote{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--muted);margin:10px 0 0}
.gate{border:1px dashed var(--broken);background:var(--broken-wash);border-radius:4px;padding:16px 20px;margin:20px 0}
.gate h3{margin-top:0;color:var(--broken)}
.gate p{font-size:16px}
.foot{margin-top:64px;padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);font-family:var(--mono);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--muted);display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px 24px}
</style>
<div class="wrap">

<header class="mast">
  <div class="eyebrow">
    <span>Work order</span>
    <span>awsTutorial · UE 5.4.1</span>
    <span>2026-08-25 · rev. 3</span>
  </div>
  <h1>Widget Repair Work Order
    <span class="sub">Vietnam needs two titles, one sign, and eighteen buttons hidden.</span>
  </h1>
  <p class="standfirst">The swap is now verified at the byte level. Vietnam's content entry turns out far smaller than the 50-button template suggests: the station was rebuilt for exactly one procedure and one interview, and the remaining buttons were never wired to anything.</p>
</header>

<div class="col">

<h2>Where things stand</h2>

</div>

<div class="scroll">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Station</th><th>Trigger now points at</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Paraguay</td><td><code>BP_SC_T1_C</code> / <code>BP_WG_TEST_C</code></td><td class="ok">verified · 360 + interviews play</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cataract Vietnam</td><td><code>BP_SC_CatViet_Test_C</code> / <code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test_C</code></td><td class="bad">verified · needs 2 titles + 1 sign</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cataract UCI-02</td><td>unchanged</td><td class="ok">the control — must still work</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>BP_WG_CAT_PARA</code>, <code>BP_WG_CAT_VIET</code></td><td>—</td><td>retired, not repaired</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="col">
<p class="tnote">Read from the <code>__ExternalActors__</code> property blocks of <code>Hospital_Client</code> — 11 orbit triggers across 4,519 actor packages. The nine untouched stations each resolve to their own screen and widget.</p>

<p class="tnote">Paraguay's null POV variables (<code>MP_360_POV_CAT_PARA_1</code>/<code>_2</code>) were already repaired and verified — zero <code>Accessed None</code> in the last run. That change is worth keeping even though the widget is being retired.</p>

<h2><span class="ph">00</span>Backup — done</h2>

<p>Taken this session, before any edit, at
<code>UCI_Campus_Template_v1.0/.backups/20260825T112304/</code> — 45 MB, 4,527 files:
<code>Hospital_Client.umap</code>, its full <code>__ExternalActors__</code> tree (4,519 packages),
the five Blueprints in play, and the two media sources named in B·5.</p>

<div class="warn">IT SITS AT THE PROJECT ROOT, NOT INSIDE Content/. The editor scans Content/ for assets and would try to import a backup copy placed there.</div>

<p>Work on the PC throughout. The Mac is Phase E and only receives the result.</p>

<h2><span class="ph">A</span>Blueprint repairs — not required</h2>

<p class="lede">This phase originally held two fixes: the interview readiness race in <code>BP_WG_TEST</code> and the open-deadlock in <code>BP_WG_CAT_VIET</code>. Both evaporated when the stations were swapped. Recorded here so the reasoning survives, not as work to do.</p>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">A · 1</span><h3>Interview race — resolved by the swap</h3></div>
  <p>The <code>MP_ITV-01 is not ready</code> failure travelled with the old widget, not with <code>BP_WG_TEST</code>. Once Paraguay pointed at <code>BP_SC_T1</code>, interviews played correctly with no graph change.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">A · 2</span><h3>Vietnam deadlock — superseded, and my diagnosis was wrong</h3></div>
  <p>I reported that <code>BP_WG_CAT_VIET</code> checked readiness <em>before</em> opening. Its graph actually does the right thing — <code>Open Source</code> first, branch on the return value, bind <code>OnMediaOpened</code>, and poll on a 3-second timer as a safety net.</p>
  <p>Two measurement errors fed that: the "zero opens" finding came from grepping <code>IMediaPlayer::Open</code>, an <strong>Electra-only</strong> line, while <code>MP_2D_CAT_VIET</code> is an mp4 that goes through <strong>WmfMedia</strong> on Windows; and the captured failure string <code>MP_2D_CAT_VIETNot Ready</code> came from the 2D path, not the 360 graph I was reasoning about.</p>
  <p>All Vietnam content is reachable — both adaptive manifests and every mp4 tier return HTTP 206 with correct MIME types — so the fault was the pre-ABR widget's own player handling. Moot now that it is retired.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="ph">B</span>Swap both stations — done, and verified</h2>

<p class="lede">Neither old widget is being repaired. <code>BP_WG_CAT_PARA</code> is the pre-ABR design — clicking a procedure opens a resolution panel and binds four quality buttons that no longer exist, so the custom event holding every <code>Open Source</code> call can never fire. <code>BP_WG_CAT_VIET</code> is the same generation. Both have adaptive-bitrate rebuilds that already work.</p>

<p>The screen-to-widget pairing lives on the <code>BP_OrbitTrigger</code> actor instance in the map, not in either Blueprint. That made this a small edit.</p>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">B · 1</span><h3>The rebuilt stations are in <code>Hospital_Client</code> — done</h3></div>
  <p>Both screen actors were <strong>converted in place</strong> rather than pasted alongside the old ones. Each kept its original object name and label while its class changed. Four external-actor packages were rewritten, 2026-08-24 18:14–18:34.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">B · 2</span><h3>Triggers repointed — done</h3></div>
  <p>Decoded from each trigger's serialized property block:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Trigger property</th><th>Paraguay</th><th>Vietnam</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>ScreenActorRef</code> → object</td><td><code>BP_SC_CAT_PARA_C_UAID_E89C25A80DC62D5902</code></td><td><code>BP_SC_CAT_VIET_C_UAID_001FBC137C7D670702</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ScreenActorRef</code> → <strong>class</strong></td><td class="ok"><code>BP_SC_T1_C</code></td><td class="ok"><code>BP_SC_CatViet_Test_C</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>WidgetClass</code></td><td class="ok"><code>BP_WG_TEST_C</code></td><td class="ok"><code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test_C</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ActorLabel</code> <span class="tnote">cosmetic</span></td><td><code>BP_OrbitTrigger_CatPara</code></td><td><code>BP_OrbitTrigger_CatViet</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The old names in the first row are what a name-table scan reports, and they read like a half-finished swap. They are not. <strong>The class is what says which station an actor is</strong>, and both classes are the rebuilds.</p>
  <div class="warn">ScreenActorRef is an ACTOR pointer — it cannot cross maps. It resolves here to a live actor inside this map's PersistentLevel, so the hazard did not bite. If a swapped station ever stops opening its menu on overlap, this is the first thing to check.</div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">B · 3</span><h3>Delete the retired actors — moot</h3></div>
  <p>There are none. The conversion replaced the old actors instead of adding new ones beside them: nothing in <code>Hospital_Client</code> still carries <code>BP_SC_CAT_PARA_C</code> or <code>BP_SC_CAT_VIET_C</code> as its class.</p>
  <p>What is left is cosmetic — the two <code>ActorLabel</code>s above, and the screen actors' own labels (<code>BP_SC_CAT_PARA</code>, <code>BP_SC_CAT_VIET</code>). Worth renaming purely so the next person is not misled.</p>
  <p>The orphaned Vietnam trigger — the one in FirstPersonMap with a widget set and no screen — needs no action. It exists only in FirstPersonMap, and Phase D overwrites that map from Hospital_Client, so it disappears on its own.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">B · 4</span><h3>Vietnam content entry — the only real work left</h3></div>
  <p>Vietnam has <strong>one procedure and one interview</strong>. That is not an oversight in the rebuild — it is what the station was built for, and two independent sources agree:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>BP_SC_CatViet_Test</code>'s defaults declare exactly three stream sources: <code>VS_360-01</code>, <code>VS_POV-DR-01</code>, <code>ITV-01</code>.</li>
    <li><code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test</code>'s graph binds <code>OnClicked</code> on exactly two content buttons: <code>Button_VS_PRO-01</code> and <code>Button_VS_ITV-01</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The other 18 content buttons have <strong>no click handler and no graph node that reads them</strong>. They are inert leftovers of the template.</p>

  <h3>The three strings to type</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>Widget</th><th>Currently</th><th>Set to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test</code></td>
        <td><code>TextBlock_131</code><br><span class="tnote">in <code>Button_VS_PRO-01</code></span></td>
        <td class="bad"><code>Procedure Title</code></td>
        <td><code>Cataract Procedure</code><br><span class="tnote">your call — see below</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test</code></td>
        <td><code>TextBlock_19</code><br><span class="tnote">in <code>Button_VS_ITV-01</code></span></td>
        <td class="bad"><code>Procedure Title</code></td>
        <td class="ok"><code>Cataract Animation: Laser vs Manual</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>BP_SC_CatViet_Test</code></td>
        <td><code>TextRender</code><br><span class="tnote">the in-world station sign</span></td>
        <td class="bad"><code>Procedure Title Here</code></td>
        <td class="ok"><code>Cataract Operation - Vietnam</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The last two are not invented. The interview button plays <code>CAT_UCI_02_ITV-LasVsManAnim1</code>, and <code>BP_WG_CAT_UCI_02</code> already labels that exact asset <code>Cataract Animation: Laser vs Manual</code>. The station sign is the label the retired <code>BP_SC_CAT_VIET</code> carries today.</p>
  <p class="tnote">The in-world sign was not in rev. 2's scope. It is a visible placeholder on the station itself, so it belongs with the other two.</p>

  <div class="gate">
    <h3>The one editorial decision</h3>
    <p>The procedure button's title is yours. Its source is <code>CAT_VIET_360</code> plus <code>CAT_VIET_POV-DR</code>, whose files are named only <em>Cataracts - Vietnam</em> — nothing on disk says whether the surgery shown is laser or manual. UCI-02 distinguishes its two as <code>Cataract Procedure - Laser</code> and <code>Cataract Procedure - Standard</code>; if Vietnam's is one of those, name it the same way.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Hide the 18 surplus buttons</h3>
  <div class="warn">COLLAPSE THE SIZEBOX, NOT THE BUTTON. Every button sits inside its own SizeBox wrapper, and the SizeBoxes are what the ScrollBox lays out. Collapsing only the Button leaves the wrapper's fixed height behind as an empty row.</div>
  <p>Set <code>Visibility → Collapsed</code> on each SizeBox below. Keep <code>SizeBox_291</code> and <code>SizeBox_10</code> — those hold the two live buttons.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Panel</th><th>Collapse these SizeBoxes</th><th>n</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Procedures<br><span class="tnote"><code>ScrollBox_165</code></span></td>
        <td><code>SizeBox_462</code> <code>SizeBox_547</code> <code>SizeBox_4</code> <code>SizeBox_3</code> <code>SizeBox_2</code><br><span class="tnote">= <code>Button_VS_PRO-02</code> · <code>03</code> · <code>04</code> · <code>05</code> · <code>06</code></span></td>
        <td>5</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Interviews<br><span class="tnote"><code>ScrollBox_375</code></span></td>
        <td><code>SizeBox_11</code> <code>SizeBox_12</code> <code>SizeBox</code> <code>SizeBox_5</code> <code>SizeBox_6</code> <code>SizeBox_1</code> <code>SizeBox_7</code> <code>SizeBox_8</code> <code>SizeBox_9</code> <code>SizeBox_14</code> <code>SizeBox_15</code> <code>SizeBox_16</code> <code>SizeBox_17</code><br><span class="tnote">= <code>Button_VS_ITV-02</code> through <code>ITV-14</code></span></td>
        <td>13</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="warn">HIDE, DO NOT DELETE. Nothing currently reads these 18 buttons, so deletion would not reproduce the BP_WG_CAT_PARA failure — but their widget-tree variables still exist on the generated class, and hiding costs nothing. Collapsed widgets take no layout space and are not hit-testable.</div>
  <div class="done">DONE WHEN — the Procedures list shows one row, the Interviews list shows one row, no row reads "Procedure Title", and the sign in the world reads Cataract Operation - Vietnam</div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">B · 5</span><h3>The two data bugs — neither blocks the ship</h3></div>
  <p>Both assets rev. 2 flagged are <strong>orphans</strong>. A scan of all 16,023 packages in the project finds no referencer for either; both are pre-adaptive leftovers the ABR rebuild does not touch. Fix as hygiene, or leave them.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Asset</th><th>The defect</th><th>Referencers</th><th>What can be done</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>CAT_VIET_MISC</code></td>
        <td>points at a Paraguay manifest — <code>/Dash/Cat-Para/Day03/Phacoemmulsification/360/adaptive_360.mpd</code></td>
        <td class="ok">none</td>
        <td>Nothing to repoint it to — no Vietnam <em>Misc</em> manifest was ever published.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>CAT_VIET_360_4K_Low</code></td>
        <td>a <code>264_1080_6Mbps</code> encode under a 4K name and a <code>/360/4K/</code> path</td>
        <td class="ok">none</td>
        <td>Rename to <code>..._1080_High</code>, or delete with its siblings.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Seven candidate paths for a Vietnam <em>Misc</em> stream were probed against the CDN — <code>Cat-Vietnam/Misc/adaptive_{360,pov,traditional}.mpd</code>, <code>Cat-Vietnam/ITV/…</code>, <code>Cat-Vietnam/LasVsMan/…</code> — and all seven return 403. The Laser-vs-Manual content <em>does</em> have a published manifest, at <code>/Dash/CAT-UCI-02/ITV/ITV-LasVsManAnimation/adaptive_pov.mpd</code>, and Vietnam's <code>ITV-01</code> already points at it. That slot is correctly wired.</p>
  <p>On the 4K name: genuine 3840×2160 Vietnam 360 <em>does</em> exist, but only as a representation inside <code>/Dash/Cat-Vietnam/360/adaptive_360.mpd</code> — which is what the station actually plays. Only the progressive tier is misnamed.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="ph">C</span>Verify in <code>Hospital_Client</code></h2>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">C · 1</span><h3>Full pass, uncooked</h3></div>
  <pre>"D:\UE_5.4.1\Engine\Binaries\Win64\UnrealEditor.exe" ^
  "C:\Users\Dalton\Documents\Unreal_Projects\UCI_Campus_Template_v1.0\awsTutorial.uproject" ^
  Hospital_Client -game -log ^
  -LogCmds="LogMediaAssets Verbose, LogElectraPlayer Verbose, LogWmfMedia Verbose, LogMedia Verbose"</pre>
  <div class="warn">KEEP LogWmfMedia IN THE LIST. Windows mp4s go through WmfMedia, not Electra. Grepping only the Electra-only IMediaPlayer::Open produced a false "zero opens" reading once already.</div>
  <p>Walk every station that changed and one that didn't:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Paraguay</strong> — procedures and interviews</li>
    <li><strong>Cataract Vietnam</strong> — its one procedure and one interview</li>
    <li><strong>Cataract UCI-02</strong> — the control; it must still work</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="done">DONE WHEN — log shows Returning: BP_SC_T1 and Current Widget = BP_WG_TEST_C_0 at Paraguay, an OpenSource per player, Playback started at play position for each, and zero Accessed None</div>
</div>

<h2><span class="ph">D</span>Ship the Windows build</h2>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">D · 1</span><h3>Save, then Save As</h3></div>
  <p>Save the Blueprints and <code>Hospital_Client</code>. Then <strong>File → Save Current Level As… → <code>FirstPersonMap</code></strong>, overwrite. Slow — it rewrites the external-actor tree.</p>
  <p>This is also what stops the bug recurring. The old Paraguay station reached macOS precisely because <code>Hospital_Client</code> was stale relative to what shipped.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">D · 2</span><h3>Package</h3></div>
  <p><strong>Platforms → Windows → Package Project</strong>, Shipping config to match your last build. The editor Package button is safe here — the deadlock in SOP §11h is macOS-only.</p>
  <div class="warn">DISK — C: 100 GB free, D: ~67 GB, both ~98% full. A cook plus staging can want 15–20 GB.</div>
</div>

<h2><span class="ph">E</span>The Mac</h2>

<p class="lede">The Mac has every asset it needs — <code>BP_SC_T1</code>, <code>BP_WG_TEST</code>, <code>BP_SC_CatViet_Test</code>, <code>BP_WG_CatViet_Test</code> are all present and <code>BP_SC_T1</code> is already cooked into the existing build. What it does not have is the corrected maps: its <code>Hospital_Client.umap</code> (24 Jul, 07:46) and <code>FirstPersonMap.umap</code> (24 Jul, 12:03) both predate every fix.</p>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">E · 1</span><h3>Carry the fixes across</h3></div>
  <p>Two routes. Copy the corrected <code>Hospital_Client.umap</code>, its <code>__ExternalActors__</code> folder, and the two edited widget and screen assets from the PC to the Mac — or repeat Phase B in the Mac editor. Copying is faster and guarantees parity; repeating avoids any risk of a partial asset set.</p>
  <p class="tnote">The engine volume is already mounted — check <code>ls /Volumes</code> before reaching for <code>hdiutil attach</code>.</p>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="hd"><span class="n">E · 2</span><h3>Package from the CLI — never the editor button</h3></div>
  <p>On macOS the editor's Package Project button deadlocks: its cook completes, then the process hangs in <code>-[NSApplication _shouldTerminate]</code> and never exits, and UAT never reaches stage. Use <strong>SOP §11h</strong>, or the one-command path:</p>
  <pre>~/Downloads/ue541-team-package/package-awsTutorial-mac.sh</pre>
  <p>Then run the packaged app with the same verbose media flags and confirm Paraguay and Vietnam both play.</p>
  <div class="done">DONE WHEN — macOS build plays Paraguay procedures, Paraguay interviews, and Vietnam</div>
</div>

